=E2=80=9CThe plan was always to get here as soon as possible,=E2=80=9D Pinn=
ock says, =E2=80=9Cwith a bit of breathing space in case that didn=E2=80=99=
t happen =E2=80=A6 Now we have to figure out how to establish ourselves. I =
think the massive opportunity here is to join something so new but under th=
e umbrella of a very established strong brand and club. It feels as if it=
=E2=80=99s startup-like but there=E2=80=99s a nice safety net.=E2=80=9D

Having the infrastructure of the wider club behind them is a positive. They=
 have drawn on their marketing and commercial expertise to build a distinct=
 identity for their women=E2=80=99s team, giving them a different kit, brin=
ging in their own sponsors and establishing a unique matchday experience. T=
heir most recent derby against Br=C3=B8ndby drew 8,047 to Parken, an A-liga=
 record.

Secure foundations, however, take time to build. Operating in a semi-profes=
sional environment, their first season at the top has come with teething pa=
ins. They parted ways with their head coach Kasper Klarskov Nielsen after t=
hree matches and sit second from bottom after 10 fixtures. Many players con=
tinue to juggle football with work or study while trying to adapt to the ph=
ysical demands of the top flight.

=E2=80=9CIt=E2=80=99s been tough so far,=E2=80=9D the forward Maria Hovmark=
 says. =E2=80=9CIt=E2=80=99s our first year in the league and we have to ge=
t used to that. Around 80-90% of the girls have never played in the top lea=
gue so it=E2=80=99s a challenge =E2=80=A6 It=E2=80=99s also a huge challeng=
e for the club because they=E2=80=99ve never been through this as well. It=
=E2=80=99s a chance for all of us and we=E2=80=99re slowly getting there.=
=E2=80=9D

FCK=E2=80=99s rise is being played out in the context of a league working t=
o establish itself. The country has produced highly technical players such =
as Pernille Harder, and the Danish Football Association (DBU) is trying to =
develop a stronger domestic game through greater investment, increased expo=
sure and the establishment of minimum standards while also featuring in Eur=
opean competition. The champions, HB K=C3=B8ge, have returned to the Champi=
ons League for the first time since 2022 and Br=C3=B8ndby will be involved =
in the Europa Cup last 16.


=E2=80=98It=E2=80=99s been tough so far. It=E2=80=99s our first year in the=
 league and we have to get used to that.=E2=80=99 Maria Hovmark of FC Copen=
hagen takes on Br=C3=B8ndby. Photograph: Freja Borne


=E2=80=9CI expected more of an egalitarian setup because of the politics of=
 Denmark and what I=E2=80=99ve found is actually women=E2=80=99s football i=
s still at the early stages of a journey,=E2=80=9D says Pinnock, who is Sou=
th African and was previously head of women=E2=80=99s football at Bristol C=
ity. =E2=80=9CThere are a few clubs who are really pushing the envelope but=
 the majority need to lift their game. That is also an opportunity. It=E2=
=80=99s exciting to see how we can look to our neighbours and say: =E2=80=
=98How are they doing it?=E2=80=99=E2=80=9D

FCK are operating on a different model to K=C3=B8ge, who have been able to =
invest immediately on the pitch. FCK=E2=80=99s longer-term ambition is to c=
hallenge for trophies, compete in Europe and become players in the global t=
ransfer market. They also want to develop homegrown players who regard stay=
ing in Denmark as an attractive option, while bringing experienced talent, =
such as Hovmark, back home.

=E2=80=9CThe league is going more professional in terms of some teams being=
 more full-time now,=E2=80=9D Hovmark says. =E2=80=9CIt is getting more ser=
ious and I think that attracts people. That also brings the level higher.=
=E2=80=9D

The wider societal impact the club can have is key for FCK. Their =E2=80=9C=
Hall of Femmes=E2=80=9D campaign with their main partner, Andel, gives youn=
g female footballers the opportunity to be featured on banners at Parken an=
d offers two annual scholarships for players aged 14-19. FCK are also testi=
ng an academy model in which girls and boys train together according to the=
ir age, ability and development needs rather than by gender.

For now, the priority is to ensure they remain an A-Liga side. The club, ho=
wever, have an eye on on-field success and patience will not last for ever.=
 =E2=80=9CWe=E2=80=99re going to get a grace period of two or three seasons=
,=E2=80=9D Pinnock says. =E2=80=9CThen we=E2=80=99ve really got to embrace =
the FCK model, which is that we=E2=80=99ve got to be winners and we=E2=80=
=99ve got to head for the top.=E2=80=9D

