<tr><td align=3D"left" class=3D"text" style=3D"background:#FFFFFF;font-size=
:0px;padding:0 16px;word-break:break-word;"><div style=3D"font-family:Georg=
ia, serif;font-size:16px;font-weight:400;line-height:22px;text-align:left;c=
olor:#121212;">
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">For two or three years now, it=E2=80=99s b=
een standard to nod approvingly at how well-run Brighton, Brentford and Bou=
rnemouth are, and to move on. What else is there to say? It=E2=80=99s true,=
 of course =E2=80=93 these are sides who have made the most of relatively l=
imited resources. But there=E2=80=99s also an underlying assumption in the =
designation: that these are clubs who have no higher aspiration, that their=
 limit is earning praise for upper mid-table finishes.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">This season, perhaps, offers an opportunit=
y to change that. Other than Arsenal, every major club is undergoing signif=
icant transition. Manchester City may have started the season with seven st=
raight wins, but there=E2=80=99s every chance the punishment for their mult=
iple breaches of Premier League regulations takes them out of the race for =
the top five. In that context, why shouldn=E2=80=99t one of those efficient=
 smaller sides qualify for the Champions League?</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Brentford began the weekend in third and w=
ith, perhaps, a slight sense of frustration that they had drawn four games.=
 Again, they will feel they might have done rather better on Saturday; they=
 were leading Liverpool 1-0 when Yehor Yarmolyuk foolishly flicked his head=
 towards J=C3=A9r=C3=A9my Jacquet and was sent off just before half-time. <=
a href=3D"https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc=
_XG18IRyTNtZQ7fIEMgszcCSneHEBgtjhYSJYcaNsDra0BxnPgmjPp58s3H-Bi_1_WmZKeK07sm=
tcFksNkJX5Yfh1iOwpqjqEoamaOF0LAKH7C9sgeb8z9_s_1TX_P4ExYbbBitn5TZ-lCivE5wieV=
BgCbYBqCzJMTyupsVp96iemdombcPjMY0Bvvz7NyC10EC8MorWEyuH0WNxk2zt3z4q-3-rUI8T9=
2os-nw8fT-Nzu-bxGNGxuhzAaAFVlOQyEWriAjurQMxsOK2EyPbdLjnyfVFxBZ5_htUK3KPjHVn=
ybmjgxSXgKvVkLpcv94XNP2DKNCg/4v0/XvntQCprRaOoXbtfobu9XA/h7/h001.oGQpS3chCq6=
1Ngnym2S-BxYCbQsboVvl3trJWZirEQg" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">The result was =
a 2-1 defeat</a> and a sense of an opportunity missed. Bournemouth have not=
 had the easiest start under Marco Rose, but <a href=3D"https://ablink.edit=
orial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IRyTNtZQ7fIEMgszcCSneHEB=
gtjhYSJYcaNsDra0BxnPgpfnpQkMfDn4ReKyI51ew3-ZUlB32HxhTvpBOuTcA9y8iyyKhOkru6l=
