hv-vpW-moKSA2-n-0GMOnh5zdt8fkQEqF6RLfz7QWnYQQXtFq2Vw8e9Q5Do-z7-cZqwuiqWWtWQ=
qCiv9ZWZy8_k7ud590i3263zz_DfUOG6j2VRyYwVZjopFOBzc08kgI0C8a9wbej0uE_RTBTe3Q/=
4v0/XvntQCprRaOoXbtfobu9XA/h8/h001.aC_Zxnykt0ooLUzx5h1KriavAh5pyaCSEk0eMMn8=
pdQ" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">Sunday=E2=80=99s 2-0 win</a> over Sunderland=
 perhaps hints at a brighter future.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Which left Brighton to carry the charge of=
 the B clubs, which they did in emphatic style, ending a run of four derbie=
s without success <a href=3D"https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/=
u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IRyTNtZQ7fIEMgszcCSneHEBgtjhYSJYcaNsDra0BxnPg4VPbRS=
JjdqPBz5wnKq_59pm0kq5UVB_woyYHY2EjPnCq8dvmpbHRStqh5hgmpUJfxaMpst0wxj1pT9QBG=
zNpEwnOjF2obbgWNCSeuYagyjeOJewwHKoHRMhhVKVq_krKbWg3jjRv-3AZQHwVSPo9eHg6Szwk=
5IvRl5WlOT_FQG4gdRqe01F0scLRsost45PP35cRcHeXOxlOyAbNWjqUXisqFYycH-2ht0c03KE=
-qSxKKnyukzMjnQAWZpBaKoDq_qjkSSgvc2RXvo7uEa7ztg/4v0/XvntQCprRaOoXbtfobu9XA/=
h9/h001.HaeGWXBLaQipYYzV3j_JOpQBDvMOMXHiPovQbx01Wzs" style=3D"color: #0077B=
6;">with a crushing 5-0 win</a>. Crystal Palace had been extremely fortunat=
e to survive a first-half battering from Nottingham Forest last week. They =
were thoroughly outplayed in the early stages again, and this time found th=
emselves 3-0 down inside 10 minutes. Perhaps the opening goal, a cross head=
ed into his own net by Jaydee Canvot, was fortunate, but it was an error in=
duced by the intensity of Brighton=E2=80=99s start. The second goal, a perf=
ectly weighted drive from just inside the Palace half from Diego G=C3=B3mez=
, spoke of a side playing with immense confidence. Already this season, 11 =
different players have scored for Brighton in the league. They have become =
the image of a side without stars, the whole far greater than the individua=
ls who comprise it.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The difference from February, when Brighto=
n last played Palace, is stark. They lost that game 1-0, meaning they had w=
on just one of their previous 12 Premier League games. Generalised grumblin=
g had begun to curdle into calls for Fabian H=C3=BCrzeler to be sacked. Whe=
n they lost Carlos Baleba, Jan Paul van Hecke, Danny Welbeck and James Miln=
er over the summer, there were reasons to wonder whether they could keep go=
ing with the process of continuous renewal.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">There were the usual assorted signings, th=
e usual mix of Premier League experience and exotic unknowns. The 19-year-o=
ld centre-back Luka Vu=C5=A1kovi=C4=87, who spent last season on loan at Ha=
mburg from Tottenham, already looks at home (and who really thought, when S=
purs and Brighton effectively swapped centre-backs, with Van Hecke moving t=
o London, that Brighton would not end up with the better of the deal?). The=
 21-year-old Spanish midfielder Chema Andr=C3=A9s, who arrived from Stuttga=
rt, sparkled from the bench against Arsenal and has excelled in the two gam=
es since.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">But the stand-out performer this season ha=
s been a far more familiar name: Pascal Gross, who returned in January afte=
r a season and a half at Borussia Dortmund. No player in the Premier League=
 has registered more than his four assists this season. Nobody has run furt=
her. He has three goals. He averages a league-high 3.0 key passes a game. T=
o bring back a player who is now 35 has been a masterstroke.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The victory over Arsenal before the intern=
ational break suggested a team capable not merely of accumulating points ag=
ainst the league=E2=80=99s lesser lights, or even of challenging the elite,=
 but of overwhelming them. Arsenal were suffocated by the Brighton press an=
d, while a couple of low-xG long-range finishes in the first half helped, t=
he fact is that Brighton dominated the match to a remarkable extent.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The question is whether they can keep it g=
oing. Just as their transfer policy is now an accepted feature of the Premi=
er League, so too is Brighton=E2=80=99s mid-season slump. They haven=E2=80=
=99t won a game in December since 2023. Is this squad strong enough to sust=
ain the challenge? Europe seems not to be unsettling Brighton as it did on =
their first foray, or as it appears to be draining Bournemouth, Crystal Pal=
ace and Sunderland =E2=80=93 which may be a reflection on the relative qual=
ity of the Conference League.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">They are doing this without Kaoru Mitoma, =
Yankuba Minteh and Mats Wieffer, all of whom should be back at some point o=
ver the next month. That suggests the depth of this Brighton squad. It=E2=
=80=99s always a major step for a club unfamiliar with the Champions League=
 to haul themselves into the top five. At times it often feels a mental bar=
rier as much as anything.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">But if any of those B clubs are ever going=
 to break into the Champions League, they may never have a better opportuni=
ty than Brighton do this season.</p>
