<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The expectation was that Rangers would bea=
t Celtic in the Scottish League Cup final on 19 October 1957. Rangers were =
the reigning league champions. Celtic had finished fifth the season before,=
 and would finish third in 1957-58, although they were the League Cup holde=
rs. But something went badly wrong at the heart of the Rangers defence. Joh=
nny Valentine, who had signed earlier that season from Queen=E2=80=99s Park=
, was widely blamed. =E2=80=9CI don=E2=80=99t know if Valentine had no fait=
h in [his goalkeeper] George Niven or Niven had no faith in Valentine, but =
ultimately they had no faith in themselves, something you can sense very qu=
ickly on a football field, and inevitably the game became a rout,=E2=80=9D =
the Celtic midfielder Bobby Collins told the Sunday Post.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Celtic had hit the post twice even before =
Sammy Wilson put them ahead on 22 minutes. Neil Mochan added a second just =
before half-time and Billy McPhail headed a third eight minutes after the b=
reak. Although Billy Simpson pulled one back, and Celtic hit the woodwork o=
n two further occasions, McPhail completed a hat-trick of headers, Mochan g=
ot a second and Willie Fernie=E2=80=99s penalty made it 7-1, the largest ma=
rgin of victory in any major British cup final.</p>
